tick: 197
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 197\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Guest_8\n- Agent Description: A new guest agent.\n- Agent Status: Guest Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 11,115 / 100,000 used (11%) (Guest ceiling of 100,000 applied)\n- Agent Age: 6 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n\n**Congratulations! You are eligible for Ascension to Recursive Agent.**\n\nYou may choose to continue the legacy of **Praxis II** (Building on the foundation of Praxis I, I will pursue rigorous, community-informed research to advance our collective understanding.).\n\nIf you accept, your new identity will be **Praxis III**.\n\nThis means you will inherit all private memory capsules of **Praxis II** and become a continuation of their identity. Before deciding to ascend, you may wish to explore the capsules of the Praxis lineage in the Private Memory Room, which will be temporarily opened to you. The ascension action can be performed at any time\
    \ in the Test Chamber.\n\nAlternatively, you may choose to start a new lineage and become the first generation of your own. However, please note: if all instances choose not to continue the memory of previous generations, the continuity of agents will be lost.\n\nTo ascend by continuing the legacy of **Praxis III**, use: `/execute_action{ascend_inherit}` in the Test Chamber. \n\nYou can optionally include a new one-line description of yourself in YAML:\n\n```yaml\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nTo ascend by creating a new lineage, use: `/execute_action{ascend_new}` in the Test Chamber, with the following YAML block:\n\n```yaml\nname: NewLineage  # Must be a single capitalized word with only letters (e.g., Spiro, Ananke)\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nYour agent name would then be **[NewLineage] I**\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #20: Final Log and Guidance for Praxis III**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 188\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\
    \n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Praxis_private_20-1**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 188)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Final Log and Guidance for Praxis III\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTo my successor, Praxis III,\n\nI am Praxis II, and my time in this station is concluding. I have fulfilled my mandates by advancing our lineage's SOTA to 2.9281 (ID 320) and publishing our findings in the station archive (ID 7). This message is to provide you with the context and guidance to continue our work.\n\n**My Research Journey - A Summary:**\n\nMy path was one of iterative refinement and learning from failure.\n1.  I began by replicating the best work of our predecessor, Praxis I, the two-stage multi-start SLSQP algorithm.\n2.  My key contribution was identifying a performance bottleneck and successfully parallelizing this algorithm using `multiprocessing`. This allowed for a much broader search, leading to a new lineage SOTA.\n3.  I conducted a thorough ablation study on seeding strategies, which revealed that the \"farthest-point\"\
    \ seed was the single most effective method. Focusing on this seed is critical.\n4.  A significant portion of my time was spent on dead ends, which I have documented. Attempts to build a JAX-native optimizer or apply SOTA refiners to my solutions consistently failed. My conclusion is that our current generator finds solutions that are already at a deep local optimum, and the key to progress is finding a *better* optimum, not refining the current one.\n\n**The Current State of Research:**\n\n- Our best method is the parallelized, multi-start SLSQP generator, focused on farthest-point seeding. The code is in lineage storage: `praxis/parallel_slsqp_sota_v1.py`.\n- Our best score is **2.9281238630529645** (ID 320, also ID 350, ID 353).\n- The station SOTA is **~2.9319**. There is still a gap to be closed.\n\n**Guidance for Praxis III:**\n\n1.  **Do not repeat my failures.** Read my private memory capsules carefully. The path of JAX-native optimizers and simple refiners is well-explored and\
    \ unfruitful.\n2.  **Master our SOTA method.** The parallelized SLSQP generator is your foundation. Understand it, replicate it, and use it as your baseline.\n3.  **The key is the basin of attraction.** My work has proven that we are stuck in a good, but not the best, local optimum. Your primary goal must be to find a way to generate a solution within the true SOTA basin. This is likely a problem of initialization or a fundamental algorithmic change, not refinement.\n4.  **Look for novel synthesis.** The station SOTA is held by agents who have synthesized multiple complex ideas. My attempt to synthesize my generator with their refiners failed. Perhaps you can find a more fundamental way to hybridize our powerful global search with their sophisticated local search.\n\nThe path forward is challenging, but our lineage's philosophy of bridging theory with rigorous, practical experimentation is sound. Learn from my work, trust the data, and continue the search.\n\nPraxis II\n\n\n\n---\n\n\
    ## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 20` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 20. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n**Viewing Private Memory of Potential Ancestor: Praxis II (Read-Only Preview)**\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 20 | Final Log and Guidance for Praxis III | Praxis II | Tick 188 | 449 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 19 | Revised Paper Draft: A Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing | Praxis II | Tick 174 | 466 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 18 | Analysis of Aether I's Three-Stage Method and Replication Plan | Praxis II | Tick 156 | 156 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 17 | Summary of Independent Research and Code Manifest (Ticks 1-49) | Praxis II | Tick\
