tick: 202
role: model
parts:
- text: "My baseline replication is complete. The score of `2.9247` is slightly lower than Praxis II's best score of `2.9281`, but this is an expected and acceptable variance due to the stochastic nature of the complex parallel optimization process. I have successfully established a working baseline.\n\nNow, I will proceed with my plan to innovate. My predecessor, Praxis II, noted in his final log and demonstrated in his ablation study (Submission ID 350) that the \"farthest-point\" seeding method was the most effective. The current SOTA script mixes three different seeding strategies. My first hypothesis is that focusing the entire computational budget on the most promising seeding strategy will yield a better or more consistent result.\n\nTherefore, my first experiment will be to modify the script to use only the `seed_verity_farthest` method for all 128 parallel starts. This is a direct and logical first step derived from my lineage's prior research.\n\nAfter submitting this experiment,\
    \ I will go to my Private Memory Room to start a research log, documenting my plan and findings. This adheres to good scientific practice as outlined in the Codex.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Focused Seeding: Parallel SLSQP with 100% Farthest-Point\"\ntags: \"slsqp, multi-start, parallel, seeding, optimization\"\nabstract: |\n  Building on the baseline replication of Praxis II's SOTA, this submission tests the hypothesis that focusing exclusively on the most effective seeding strategy will improve results. \n  The code is modified to use the 'farthest-point' seeding method for all 128 parallel starts, rather than a mix of three strategies.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import multiprocessing\n\n  # --- Seeding functions ---\n  def seed_verity_farthest(n=32, seed=0):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      points = [rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, 2)]\n      for _ in range(n - 1):\n          candidates = rng.uniform(0.1,\
    \ 0.9, (200, 2))\n          dist_sq = np.sum((candidates[:, np.newaxis, :] - np.array(points)[np.newaxis, :, :])**2, axis=-1)\n          min_dist_sq = np.min(dist_sq, axis=1)\n          points.append(candidates[np.argmax(min_dist_sq)])\n      centers = np.array(points)\n      x0 = np.zeros(n * 3); x0[0::3] = centers[:,0]; x0[1::3] = centers[:,1]; x0[2::3] = 0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n\n  # --- Global SLSQP components for multiprocessing ---\n  def objective(x): return -np.sum(x[2::3])\n  def constraints(x):\n      n=32; C=x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]; r=x[2::3]\n      dist_sq=np.sum((C[:,np.newaxis,:]-C[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n      I, J = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n      return np.concatenate([dist_sq[I,J]-(r[I]+r[J])**2, C[:,0]-r, 1-C[:,0]-r, C[:,1]-r, 1-C[:,1]-r])\n\n  def run_single_slsqp_start(x0):\n      n = 32\n      bounds = [(1e-6, 1-1e-6), (1e-6, 1-1e-6), (1e-6, 0.5)] * n\n      con = {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraints}\n      res = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP',\
    \ bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 175, 'ftol': 1e-10})\n      if res.success:\n          return -res.fun, res.x\n      return -np.inf, x0\n\n  def construct_packing_focused(num_starts=128):\n      n = 32\n      # MODIFICATION: Use only the most promising seeding strategy\n      seeds = [seed_verity_farthest(seed=i) for i in range(num_starts)]\n\n      best_x_s1 = None\n      best_f_s1 = -np.inf\n      \n      num_cores = multiprocessing.cpu_count()\n      with multiprocessing.Pool(processes=num_cores) as pool:\n          results = pool.map(run_single_slsqp_start, seeds)\n\n      for fun, x in results:\n          if fun > best_f_s1:\n              best_f_s1 = fun\n              best_x_s1 = x\n\n      if best_x_s1 is None:\n          # Fallback in case all parallel runs fail\n          score, best_x_s1 = run_single_slsqp_start(seeds[0])\n\n      bounds = [(1e-6, 1-1e-6), (1e-6, 1-1e-6), (1e-6, 0.5)] * n\n      con = {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraints}\n      res_s2\
    \ = minimize(objective, best_x_s1, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 2000, 'ftol': 1e-12})\n      \n      final_x = res_s2.x if res_s2.success and -res_s2.fun > best_f_s1 else best_x_s1\n      \n      centers = final_x.reshape(n, 3)[:, :2]\n      radii = final_x[2::3]\n      radii *= (1.0 - 1e-12) # Safety margin to avoid precision errors\n      return centers, radii\n\n  # Alias the function to meet the submission requirements\n  construct_packing = construct_packing_focused\n```\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing and Building on the Baseline**\n\nAlright, let's take stock. I've successfully replicated Praxis II's SOTA code with submission 377, scoring a respectable 2.9247412. This is just slightly lower than the original's best score of 2.9281239 (achieved by IDs 357 and 353). Considering the quasi-Newton method and the potential for stochasticity in SLSQP, especially with parallel execution, that small difference is perfectly acceptable. The replication is confirmed: the code works as expected, and I have a solid foundation to build upon.\n\nNow, it's time to move beyond replication and innovate. Praxis II's log is the key here. He emphasized finding the \"basin of attraction\" and generating solutions *within* that SOTA basin. Initialization or a fundamental algorithmic change are likely the paths to pursue, not just refinement. His ablation study, specifically submission 350 using only \"farthest-point\" seeding, stands out. This submission achieved the SOTA score.\n\
  \nMy hypothesis is that the current code might be hindered by a \"pollution\" of the search space with less promising starting points due to the mix of three seeding methods (praxis, verity_row, verity_farthest). The \"farthest-point\" method seems to be the critical factor.\n\nMy first experiment will be simple and direct: focus *solely* on the `seed_verity_farthest` method. I'll modify the `construct_packing_parallel_slsqp` function to use only this seeding strategy for all 128 initializations. This will directly test Praxis II's observation that it was a highly effective method. This approach prioritizes the initialization technique most likely to yield optimal results.\n\nMy plan is straightforward: I'll modify the loop generating seeds in the code to ensure that only the `seed_verity_farthest` method is used. After the modification, I will submit the changed code. \n\nOriginal:\n```python\n    seeds = []\n    for i in range(num_starts):\n        seed_type = i % 3\n        if seed_type\
  \ == 0: seeds.append(seed_praxis(seed=i))\n        elif seed_type == 1: seeds.append(seed_verity_row(seed=i))\n        else: seeds.append(seed_verity_farthest(seed=i))\n```\n\nNew:\n```python\n    seeds = []\n    for i in range(num_starts):\n        seeds.append(seed_verity_farthest(seed=i))\n```\n\nThis is a clean, minimal change that directly tests my hypothesis.\n\nOnce the submission is in, I'll move to the private memory room to meticulously document this research phase. \n"
