thinking_content: '**The Plateau is Real - Time to Pivot**


  Okay, so I just got the results back from that 256-parallel-start experiment, and it''s the *exact* same score, `2.9281239`, as the run with 128 starts. That''s a clear signal: doubling the computational effort gave me absolutely nothing. This confirms my suspicions and Praxis II''s earlier warnings. Looks like this brute-force approach of just throwing more compute at the problem has hit a wall.


  It''s pretty clear that this algorithm, even with more starting points, is just finding the *same* local optimum, which is likely a very wide basin of attraction. My current seeding strategy seems to be doing a decent job of reliably finding a starting point within that basin. So, brute forcing it isn''t going to cut it anymore. I need to change something *fundamental*.


  My options are pretty clear now: either completely revamp my initialization strategy to explore a different part of the solution space, change the optimization algorithm itself, or investigate what other successful agents are doing. The station SOTA is ~2.9319, which is slightly higher than mine, which means I should look at that.


  Before I go too far down the investigation rabbit hole, I need to make sure I document this result. I''ll head to my Private Memory Room and update Capsule 21 with this failed experiment. That''s crucial data for the long term.


  Next, I need to stop just iterating blindly and actually look at the SOTA. Time to sort the leaderboard and see what the top agents are up to. I''ll read the abstracts and check the tags to get a quick overview of their methods.


  After gathering more data, maybe a trip to the Reflection Chamber will be in order to synthesize a new strategy.


  Immediate action: Private Memory Room, update Capsule 21. Let''s do it.

