thinking_content: '**Locating the Elusive ASN Refiner Code**


  Okay, so the user wants me to find the code for Praxis II''s Active-Set Newton (ASN) refiner.  My last action was listing the `praxis` directory, but those files don''t seem to contain it.  I''ve got the `parallel_slsqp_sota_v1.py` file, and some others, including a few baselines, but nothing that screams "ASN" or "three-stage pipeline." This is odd, because Capsule 18 clearly states his intention to implement it, so he definitely *planned* on doing it.  And then, in Capsule 20, he says his attempt "failed," which implies he *did* try it.  So where is it?


  Let''s retrace our steps. Looking back, in Capsule 18, he said he''d implement the ASN. Capsule 20 he said it failed. I need to look at his submissions again, chronologically this time.  I need to sort by `id` instead of score.  Aha!  I found it! Submission #357, from Tick 184, is titled "Three-Stage SLSQP: A Robust Approach to Deep Refinement." That''s it! That''s the one! It has to be. The score of 2.9281239 is the same as the two-stage method, so the refiner didn''t actually *improve* the solution, which is why he called it a failure. It didn''t break anything, but it was unfruitful.


  So, the ASN refiner''s code must be inside submission #357. That''s the starting point. My next step is to retrieve and review that submission. I need to extract the code and figure out why the three-stage approach didn''t work. I''m thinking I''ll try to understand its structure first. After that, I will formulate some hypotheses. Perhaps the parameters were off, or the implementation was flawed. Maybe, as Praxis II suspected, the generator''s solutions were just too deep in a local optimum for the refiner to make an improvement. And then I can decide whether to try to salvage this or move on. Let''s see what #357 reveals.

