- text: "Let me refine the experimental plan. My goal is to test the physics generator concept, but I should do it in a way that leverages my existing successful architecture. The multi-start parallel approach is powerful because it explores multiple starting points. I should not abandon that.\n\n**Revised Experimental Plan:**\n\n1.  **Create a hybrid generator function.** This function, let's call it `run_single_physics_simulation(seed)`, will take a random seed, initialize 32 circles randomly, and run the gradient descent simulation for a set number of steps. It will return the final set of center coordinates.\n\n2.  **Integrate into the multi-start framework.** Instead of `seed_verity_farthest`, the main function will call `run_single_physics_simulation` in parallel for each of the 128 starts.\n    *   This means I'll be running 128 independent physics simulations to generate 128 different initial configurations.\n    *   Each of these 128 configurations will then be passed to the *first\
    \ stage* of the SLSQP optimizer, also in parallel.\n\n3.  **The rest of the pipeline remains the same.** The best result from the 128 parallel SLSQP runs will be selected and then passed to the deep refinement (Stage 2) and ultra-deep refinement (Stage 3) SLSQP solvers.\n\n**Why this is a better plan:**\n\n*   **Robustness:** By running 128 independent simulations, I am not betting on a single simulation run finding a good basin. I am sampling 128 different \"settled\" states, increasing the chance that at least one of them is in a superior basin of attraction.\n*   **Direct Comparison:** It reuses the proven and powerful parallel optimization framework. The only change is the method of generating the initial seeds. This makes the experiment a clean test of the physics-based generator versus the farthest-point generator.\n*   **Time Management:** This is a potential issue. 128 full simulations plus 128 SLSQP runs could be too slow. I need to be careful with the `num_steps` in the simulation.\
    \ I should start with a relatively small number, maybe 200-300 steps, and see if it times out. I can analyze the logs to see where the time is being spent.\n\n**The First Submission:**\n\nThe code will be a modification of my three-stage SOTA script (ID 357).\n*   The `seed_farthest_point` function will be replaced with the new `run_single_physics_simulation` logic.\n*   The main loop will call this new function to generate the `seeds`.\n*   The title and abstract will clearly state this is a test of a new physics-based generator.\n\nThis feels like a solid, well-motivated plan that directly addresses the conclusions from my previous experiments. It is a significant but controlled leap into a new research direction."
