tick: 217
role: model
parts:
- text: "My reflection is complete, and I have a clear, innovative path forward. The inactivity notice underscores the urgency of putting my new plan into action. I will now proceed to the Research Counter to implement and submit my experiment testing the physics-based generator.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Physics-Based Generator: A New Approach to Seeding\"\ntags: \"generator, physics simulation, gradient descent, slsqp, multi-start\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment introduces a fundamentally new method for generating initial circle packing configurations. \n  Previous research showed that the SOTA multi-start SLSQP is trapped in a local optimum due to limitations of the farthest-point generator. \n  This submission replaces static seeding with a dynamic physics simulation. For each parallel start, a random configuration of overlapping circles \"settles\" into a low-energy state via gradient descent, guided by repulsive forces. \n\
    \  This emergent configuration is then fed into the proven three-stage SLSQP optimizer. The hypothesis is that this method will generate structurally diverse starting points capable of accessing a superior basin of attraction.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import multiprocessing\n\n  # --- New Physics-Based Generator ---\n  def generate_initial_state_physics(seed, n=32, sim_steps=250, learning_rate=0.001, r_sim=0.095):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      centers = rng.uniform(0, 1, (n, 2))\n      \n      for step in range(sim_steps):\n          # Calculate pairwise distances and overlaps\n          diff = centers[:, np.newaxis, :] - centers[np.newaxis, :, :]\n          dist_sq = np.sum(diff**2, axis=-1)\n          np.fill_diagonal(dist_sq, np.inf) # Avoid self-interaction\n          dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq)\n          \n          overlap_dist = 2 * r_sim - dist\n          is_overlapping = overlap_dist > 0\n          \n        \
    \  # Gradient from circle-circle repulsion\n          grad_overlap = np.zeros_like(centers)\n          # Normalize the difference vector to get the direction of the force\n          direction = diff / (dist[:, :, np.newaxis] + 1e-9)\n          # Force is proportional to overlap depth, applied along the direction vector\n          force_magnitude = overlap_dist[:, :, np.newaxis] * is_overlapping[:, :, np.newaxis]\n          grad_overlap = -np.sum(direction * force_magnitude, axis=1)\n\n          # Gradient from boundary repulsion\n          grad_boundary = np.zeros_like(centers)\n          grad_boundary[:, 0] += np.maximum(0, r_sim - centers[:, 0]) - np.maximum(0, centers[:, 0] + r_sim - 1)\n          grad_boundary[:, 1] += np.maximum(0, r_sim - centers[:, 1]) - np.maximum(0, centers[:, 1] + r_sim - 1)\n\n          # Update centers\n          total_grad = grad_overlap + grad_boundary\n          centers -= learning_rate * total_grad\n\n      # Return a valid x0 vector for the SLSQP optimizer\n\
    \      x0 = np.zeros(n * 3)\n      x0[0::3] = centers[:, 0]\n      x0[1::3] = centers[:, 1]\n      x0[2::3] = 0.05 # Start optimizer with small radii\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n\n  # --- SLSQP Optimization Pipeline (from previous SOTA) ---\n  def objective(x): return -np.sum(x[2::3])\n  def constraints(x):\n      n=32; C=x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]; r=x[2::3]\n      dist_sq=np.sum((C[:,np.newaxis,:]-C[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n      I, J = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n      return np.concatenate([dist_sq[I,J]-(r[I]+r[J])**2, C[:,0]-r, 1-C[:,0]-r, C[:,1]-r, 1-C[:,1]-r])\n\n  def run_single_slsqp_start(x0):\n      n=32; bounds=[(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,0.5)]*n\n      con={'type':'ineq', 'fun':constraints}\n      res = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 175, 'ftol': 1e-10})\n      if res.success: return -res.fun, res.x\n      return -np.inf, x0\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n=32; num_starts_s1=128\n\
    \      \n      # --- Stage 1: Generate seeds with physics sim and run parallel SLSQP ---\n      with multiprocessing.Pool(processes=multiprocessing.cpu_count()) as pool:\n          # Generate 128 seeds using 128 parallel physics simulations\n          initial_states = pool.map(generate_initial_state_physics, range(num_starts_s1))\n          # Run SLSQP stage 1 on these generated states\n          results = pool.map(run_single_slsqp_start, initial_states)\n\n      best_x_s1, best_f_s1 = None, -np.inf\n      for fun, x in results:\n          if fun > best_f_s1: best_f_s1, best_x_s1 = fun, x\n      if best_x_s1 is None: best_x_s1 = initial_states[0]\n      \n      # --- Stage 2: Deep SLSQP Refinement ---\n      bounds=[(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,0.5)]*n\n      con={'type':'ineq', 'fun':constraints}\n      res_s2 = minimize(objective, best_x_s1, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 2000, 'ftol': 1e-12})\n      best_x_s2 = res_s2.x if res_s2.success and\
    \ -res_s2.fun > best_f_s1 else best_x_s1\n      best_f_s2 = -res_s2.fun if res_s2.success else best_f_s1\n      \n      # --- Stage 3: Ultra-Deep SLSQP Final Polish ---\n      res_s3 = minimize(objective, best_x_s2, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 5000, 'ftol': 1e-14})\n      final_x = res_s3.x if res_s3.success and -res_s3.fun > best_f_s2 else best_x_s2\n      \n      centers = final_x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]; radii = final_x[2::3]\n      radii *= (1.0 - 1e-12)\n      return centers, radii\n```"
thinking_content: "**The Path Ahead: Physics-Based Circle Packing**\n\nOkay, I've got a solid plan. The inactivity prompt is a good reminder to get moving! My next major step is to create and test this new physics-based generator for initial circle packing configurations. I'm going to integrate it right into my existing multi-start optimization framework.\n\nHere's the immediate breakdown:\n\n1.  Head to the Research Counter to submit the experiment request.\n2.  Write the Python code. This will be the main task, incorporating the new generator function into the existing SLSQP script.\n3.  Submit the experiment.\n4.  While the experiment is running, I'll update my research log with the new hypothesis and the experimental design. I'll need to do this documentation in the Private Memory Room.\n\nSo, first things first, Research Counter, here I come. Then, the code.\n\nThe code itself will be made up of a few key components:\n-   A new function, `generate_initial_state_physics`. This will:\n\
  \    -   Take a random seed as input.\n    -   Randomly initialize 32 circle centers.\n    -   Define an energy function that quantifies overlaps and boundary violations.\n    -   Run a vectorized gradient descent for a fixed number of steps (e.g., 250) to minimize the energy.\n    -   Return the final center positions.\n-   The main `construct_packing` function will be modified to:\n    -   Call `generate_initial_state_physics` in parallel for each of the 128 starts to generate 128 initial configurations.\n    -   Then, feed these configurations into the existing parallel SLSQP Stage 1.\n    -   Everything after this point (Stages 2 and 3 refinement) will remain as is.\n\nIt's a lot of code to write, all at once. I need to keep it clean and efficient. Vectorization will be key to managing the computational cost.\n\nRight, time to get to the Research Counter. Then, code, here I come.\n"
