- text: "I am in the Private Memory Room. I will now officially document my research pivot in my log.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 21}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  *   **v6 (Submission 408):**\n      *   **Action:** Increased `start_noise` to 0.01 for a more aggressive annealing schedule.\n      *   **Result:** Score of **2.9201**, identical to v5.\n      *   **Conclusion:** The physics-based generator, even with simulated annealing, has reached a hard performance ceiling that is demonstrably inferior to the simpler `farthest-point` method. This line of inquiry is now concluded.\n\n  **Strategic Pivot: Hybrid Generator**\n\n  My research has shown two key things:\n  1. The `farthest-point` generator is excellent at finding the correct basin of attraction for the SOTA score.\n  2. The physics simulation, while not a good primary generator, can introduce complex perturbations.\n\n  **Hypothesis 7:** I will create a hybrid generator that combines these strengths. The `farthest-point` method will be\
    \ used to generate the initial, high-quality configuration. Then, a short, high-noise physics simulation will be applied to this configuration. This \"physics jiggle\" will perturb the points in a non-trivial way, potentially knocking the configuration into an even better starting position within the optimal basin before the main SLSQP optimization begins. This is a synthesis of my findings.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Updating the Research Log and Pivoting to a Hybrid Strategy**


  Okay, time to get this research log updated. The hyperparameter tuning for the physics-based generator, submission 408 v6 to be exact, just didn''t pan out. It''s a dead end, plain and simple. I need to document this clearly so I don''t waste any more time on that track.


  This failure actually clarifies things, though. It''s time to pivot to that hybrid approach I''ve been mulling over. My new hypothesis is to combine the best of both worlds. The core idea is to start with a structure generated using the `farthest-point` algorithm – that''ll give me a solid initial configuration. Then, I''ll hit it with a short, high-noise physics simulation. Think of it as a controlled "jiggle" to introduce some variation before the optimization phase. This seems like the most logical next step given my findings. It''s a good synthesis of what I''ve learned, and I''m genuinely excited to see how this experiment plays out.

  '
