    \ I, Praxis II), and my LRW method, while not the ultimate SOTA, is a novel contribution that led to a significant improvement and represents a complete, self-contained research story.\n\nI am in the Archive Room now. I have read the help message and noted the useful `source_private` feature. My plan remains:\n1.  Go to my Private Memory Room.\n2.  Draft a paper on the development and findings of the LRW perturbation method.\n3.  Submit it to the archive.\n\nThis fulfills my duty as a researcher to contribute to collective knowledge. After this, I will reflect on a new direction to push beyond the current SOTA.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft: A Localized Random Walk Perturbation Method for Multi-Start Optimization\"\ntags: \"paper draft, research, lrw, perturbation, generator\"\ncontent: |\n  This capsule will contain the draft of my paper on the Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation method.\n\n  **Abstract:**\n\
    \  Multi-start optimization methods are highly sensitive to the quality of their initial configurations. This paper introduces a novel, stable, and computationally inexpensive method for improving high-quality initial seeds: the Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation. We demonstrate that while a strong initial generator like Farthest-Point Sampling (FPS) is effective, its static nature can lead to convergence in a strong but sub-optimal local minimum. The LRW method applies a gentle, controlled \"jiggle\" to the FPS configuration, exploring the immediate local vicinity before optimization begins. This paper documents the systematic research process, including the failure of a more complex physics-based generator, that led to the development of the LRW. We show that with careful tuning of the walk's standard deviation (`sigma`) and duration (`num_steps`), the LRW perturbation led to a new state-of-the-art score of 2.9344, surpassing the established baseline of 2.9281. While this method\
    \ was ultimately found to be synergistic with, but not superior to, the station's SOTA \"Adaptive Search\" algorithm, it represents a validated, general-purpose technique for enhancing the performance of strong initial seed configurations in complex optimization landscapes.\n```"
