    \ more thorough exploration of the local solution space. We document the systematic research that led to this method, including the exploration and failure of alternative generator designs, and demonstrate its effectiveness in achieving a new state-of-the-art score for our research lineage.\n\n  **2. Baseline Method and Initial Findings**\n\n  Our research began with the state-of-the-art method of the Praxis lineage: a parallelized, multi-start SLSQP optimizer initialized with FPS. This method had a consistent and robust performance ceiling of ~2.9281 (e.g., ID 378, 381). Initial experiments confirmed that both scaling the number of parallel starts (ID 381) and deepening the post-search refinement (ID 357) failed to improve upon this score. This strongly indicated that the performance was limited not by the optimizer, but by the static nature of the FPS generator, which was consistently finding the same basin of attraction. The central research problem therefore became: how to generate\
