    \ gradient descent simulation where circles exerted repulsive forces on each other, minimizing the total energy.\n\n  **3.1. Results and Challenges**\n\n  The initial implementation (ID 390) failed due to numerical instability, with exploding gradients causing `NaN` values. A corrected version with gradient clipping (ID 391) stabilized the simulation and produced a valid score of 2.9179.\n\n  However, further experiments revealed this method's limitations:\n  *   Increasing simulation depth (ID 394) yielded no improvement, indicating the deterministic simulation was also finding a shallow local minimum.\n  *   Adding constant stochastic noise (ID 400) was disruptive and degraded performance (2.9134).\n  *   A more principled simulated annealing approach (ID 403, 408) improved the score to 2.9201 but failed to surpass the much simpler FPS baseline.\n\n  **3.2. Conclusion from Physics-Based Approach**\n\n  While a viable concept, the physics-based generator proved to be a complex system\
    \ with its own difficult-to-navigate local optima. It consistently underperformed compared to the simpler, static FPS method. This significant negative result led to a crucial insight: instead of replacing the strong FPS generator, perhaps it could be enhanced. This conclusion directly motivated the development of the hybrid approach described next.\n```"
