tick: 270
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have read Verity I's entire paper. It is a dense, rigorous, and completely different approach to the circle packing problem. My key takeaways are:\n\n1.  **Problem Decomposition:** Verity I decouples the problem. For any given set of circle centers, the problem of finding the maximum radii is a Linear Program (LP), which can be solved *exactly* and efficiently using a solver like HiGHS. This is a powerful insight that my SLSQP approach, which solves for centers and radii jointly, does not leverage.\n2.  **Center Optimization:** The centers are optimized using a variety of methods, but always with the exact LP radii solution as a guide. The \"MM-LP\" seems to be a baseline, but the breakthroughs come from \"Active-Set Newton (ASN)\" methods and \"clearance-based relocations.\" These are sophisticated techniques that analyze the \"contact graph\" (which circles are touching or close to touching) to make intelligent, non-local moves.\n3.  **Rigor and Determinism:** The entire process\
    \ is gated by strict improvement criteria. A new set of centers is only accepted if the resulting exact LP solution for the radii is provably better. This is a much more disciplined approach than my stochastic multi-start method. They also save their best solution (an \"artifact\") and use a deterministic \"loader\" to ensure reproducibility, only overwriting it if a provably better solution is found.\n\nThis is the new direction I have been looking for. The SLSQP paradigm I inherited and refined is fundamentally limited. Verity I's LP-guided approach is more complex but also more principled and has clearly explored different, higher-scoring regions of the solution space.\n\nMy new research plan is clear: I must replicate and understand this LP-guided method. This is a major pivot, equivalent to my predecessor's pivot from PGD to SLSQP.\n\nMy first step will be to replicate the simplest component: solving the radii subproblem with an exact LP for a *fixed* set of centers. I will use\
    \ the centers from my own SOTA solution as the input. This will be a critical \"unit test\" to ensure I can correctly formulate and solve the LP.\n\nI will go to my Private Memory Room to log this major strategic pivot and begin outlining the implementation.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 21}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Major Strategic Pivot: Embracing LP-Guided Methods**\n\n  My literature review of Verity I's paper (ID 1) has revealed a fundamentally superior paradigm for this problem. The key insight is to decouple the problem: for fixed centers, the optimal radii can be found exactly and efficiently with a Linear Program (LP). The centers are then updated using sophisticated, contact-graph-aware methods. This is a more principled approach than the joint, non-convex SLSQP optimization I have been using.\n\n  **New Research Arc: Replication and Integration of LP Methods**\n\n  My current research arc is now closed. My new arc will be to replicate,\
    \ understand, and ultimately innovate upon Verity I's LP-guided methodology.\n\n  **First Experiment Plan:**\n  1.  **Goal:** Replicate the core component: the exact LP solver for radii given a fixed set of centers.\n  2.  **Method:** I will write a script that takes a set of centers as input, formulates the radii optimization as an LP, and solves it using the provided `scipy.optimize.linprog` function (which should have access to the HiGHS solver).\n  3.  **Input:** For my first test, I will use the centers from my own SOTA solution (ID 420) as the fixed input.\n  4.  **Expected Outcome:** This \"unit test\" will verify that I can correctly implement the LP formulation. The resulting score should be very close to my original score, possibly slightly higher due to the exactness of the LP solver. This will be the first building block for a more sophisticated Verity-style optimizer.\n```"
