parts:
- text: "Excellent. My unit test was a complete success. The LP solver, running on the fixed centers from my SOTA artifact, produced a score of `2.9344937302385077`, which is identical to the original score within a very tight tolerance. This validates two critical things:\n1.  My SOTA artifact was saved correctly and is reproducible.\n2.  My formulation of the linear program for the radii subproblem is correct.\n\nI now have the foundational building block for Verity I's methodology. The next step is to move from a static analysis to a dynamic optimization.\n\nAccording to Verity I's paper, their simplest center-optimization method is \"MM-LP\" (Majorization-Minimization with Linear Programming). It involves linearizing the non-convex distance constraints and solving a trust-region-constrained LP to find an update direction for the centers. After each center update, the exact LP for the radii is re-solved to determine if the move was beneficial.\n\nThis is a complex step. Before attempting\
    \ a full implementation, I will implement a single iteration of this process as my next unit test.\n\n**Plan for Next Experiment:**\n1.  Load my SOTA centers.\n2.  Solve the exact LP for the initial radii (as I just did).\n3.  Perform **one** MM-LP step to calculate a proposed update `delta_c` for the centers.\n4.  Apply the update: `centers_new = centers_old + delta_c`.\n5.  Solve the exact LP for the radii again using `centers_new`.\n6.  The final score will tell me if a single step of this method can produce any improvement at all.\n\nThis incremental approach adheres to my new, more robust experimental protocol. First, I will update my research log.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 21}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **LP-Guided Methods, Step 1: Success**\n\n  *   **Unit Test (ID 457):** Successfully implemented and validated an exact LP solver for the radii subproblem. Using the fixed centers from my SOTA artifact (ID 420), the LP solver reproduced the\
    \ score of `2.9344937302385077`, confirming the correctness of my LP formulation.\n\n  *   **Next Step (Unit Test 2):** I will now implement a single step of the Majorization-Minimization LP (MM-LP) method for updating the centers. This involves linearizing the distance constraints to create an LP that solves for a center-update vector. I will apply this update to my SOTA centers and re-solve for the radii with my validated LP solver. This will test the core mechanic of Verity I's center-optimization strategy in a controlled, single-step experiment.\n```"
