tick: 277
role: model
parts:
- text: "My research log is updated. I will now implement the iterative MM-LP optimizer, correctly incorporating the dual variables from the radii LP. This is a complex but crucial step. I will start with my SOTA artifact and run the optimizer for 20 iterations. Given my high token usage, this focused, single-threaded experiment is the most responsible way to test this new, complex algorithm.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Iterative MM-LP with Duals (20 Iterations)\"\ntags: \"lp, mm-lp, verity replication, dual variables, optimization\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the first full implementation of an iterative LP-guided method, based on Verity I's work. It builds upon my validated LP solvers. The algorithm starts with my SOTA artifact and iterates 20 times. In each iteration, it: 1) solves an exact LP for the radii and extracts the dual variables for the active constraints; 2) uses these duals to construct an objective function for a second\
    \ LP that solves for an optimal update to the center coordinates within a trust region; 3) applies the center update; and 4) re-solves for the final radii. This experiment tests the core mechanics of a dual-informed iterative optimizer.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n\n  def solve_radii_lp(centers, return_duals=False):\n      N_CIRCLES = centers.shape[0]\n      c = -np.ones(N_CIRCLES)\n      A_ub, b_ub = [], []\n      \n      num_boundary_cons = 4 * N_CIRCLES\n      for i in range(N_CIRCLES):\n          cx, cy = centers[i]\n          for j, val in [(i, cx), (i, 1 - cx), (i, cy), (i, 1 - cy)]:\n              row = np.zeros(N_CIRCLES); row[j] = 1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(val)\n              \n      indices = np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES, k=1)\n      for i, j in zip(*indices):\n          dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n          row = np.zeros(N_CIRCLES); row[i] = 1; row[j] = 1\n          A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(dist)\n\n\
    \      res = linprog(c, A_ub=np.array(A_ub), b_ub=np.array(b_ub), bounds=(0, None), method='highs')\n      \n      if not res.success:\n          return (np.zeros(N_CIRCLES), None) if return_duals else np.zeros(N_CIRCLES)\n\n      if return_duals:\n          # Duals correspond to the inequality constraints\n          duals = res.dual\n          boundary_duals = duals[:num_boundary_cons]\n          pairwise_duals = duals[num_boundary_cons:]\n          return res.x, (boundary_duals, pairwise_duals)\n      else:\n          return res.x\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      N_CIRCLES = 32\n      MAX_ITER = 20\n      \n      try:\n          artifact = np.load('storage/praxis/sota_artifact_420.npz')\n          centers = artifact['centers']\n      except FileNotFoundError:\n          centers = np.random.rand(N_CIRCLES, 2)\n\n      for it in range(MAX_ITER):\n          radii, duals = solve_radii_lp(centers, return_duals=True)\n          if duals is None: break\n\n          boundary_duals,\
    \ pairwise_duals = duals\n          \n          # Objective for MM-LP: Maximize predicted radii gain\n          # c_mm^T * delta_c, where c_mm is assembled from duals\n          c_mm = np.zeros(2 * N_CIRCLES)\n          \n          # Boundary dual contributions\n          for i in range(N_CIRCLES):\n              c_mm[2*i]   += boundary_duals[4*i] - boundary_duals[4*i+1] # -1*dual_xneg, +1*dual_xpos\n              c_mm[2*i+1] += boundary_duals[4*i+2] - boundary_duals[4*i+3] # -1*dual_yneg, +1*dual_ypos\n\n          # Pairwise dual contributions\n          indices = np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES, k=1)\n          for k, (i, j) in enumerate(zip(*indices)):\n              diff = centers[i] - centers[j]\n              dist = np.linalg.norm(diff)\n              if dist < 1e-9: continue\n              grad = diff / dist\n              \n              c_mm[2*i]   -= pairwise_duals[k] * grad[0]\n              c_mm[2*i+1] -= pairwise_duals[k] * grad[1]\n              c_mm[2*j]   += pairwise_duals[k]\
    \ * grad[0]\n              c_mm[2*j+1] += pairwise_duals[k] * grad[1]\n\n          # Linearized constraints are the same as before\n          A_ub_mm, b_ub_mm = [], []\n          for i in range(N_CIRCLES):\n              row_x = np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES); row_x[2*i] = 1; A_ub_mm.append(row_x); b_ub_mm.append(1-centers[i,0]-radii[i])\n              row_nx = np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES); row_nx[2*i] = -1; A_ub_mm.append(row_nx); b_ub_mm.append(centers[i,0]-radii[i])\n              row_y = np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES); row_y[2*i+1] = 1; A_ub_mm.append(row_y); b_ub_mm.append(1-centers[i,1]-radii[i])\n              row_ny = np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES); row_ny[2*i+1] = -1; A_ub_mm.append(row_ny); b_ub_mm.append(centers[i,1]-radii[i])\n\n          indices = np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES, k=1)\n          for i, j in zip(*indices):\n              diff = centers[i] - centers[j]; dist = np.linalg.norm(diff)\n              if dist < 1e-9: continue\n              grad = diff / dist; row = np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES)\n           \
    \   row[2*i]=-grad[0]; row[2*i+1]=-grad[1]; row[2*j]=grad[0]; row[2*j+1]=grad[1]\n              A_ub_mm.append(row); b_ub_mm.append(dist-(radii[i]+radii[j]))\n\n          trust_radius = 0.005\n          bounds_mm = [(-trust_radius, trust_radius)] * (2 * N_CIRCLES)\n          \n          res_mm = linprog(-c_mm, A_ub=np.array(A_ub_mm), b_ub=np.array(b_ub_mm), bounds=bounds_mm, method='highs')\n\n          if res_mm.success:\n              delta_c = res_mm.x.reshape(N_CIRCLES, 2)\n              centers = centers + delta_c\n          else:\n              break # Stop if we can't find an improvement direction\n\n      final_radii = solve_radii_lp(centers)\n      return centers, final_radii * (1.0 - 1e-12)\n```"
